      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.40564",
      "date": "2026-11-02",
      "authors": [
        "Yuda Song",
        "Alvin Zhang",
        "Fahim Tajwar",
        "Daman Arora",
        "Jeff Schneider",
        "Daniel Khashabi",
        "Ruslan Salakhutdinov",
        "J. Andrew Bagnell",
        "Aarti Singh",
        "Andrea Zanette"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this paper to discover how pretraining, fine-tuning, and reinforcement learning can be mathematically unified into a single likelihood-based training algorithm. You will learn how viewing model training as dynamic data selection improves compute efficiency and task coverage.",
      "hn_id": "50336490",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50336490",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Separating foundation model training into disjoint stages of pretraining, supervised fine-tuning, and reinforcement learning is an artifact of tooling rather than a mathematical necessity.\n\nA unified framework rederives reinforcement learning objectives as maximum likelihood estimation over model generated distributions. Instead of switching optimizers and loss formulations between phases, the entire training pipeline runs under one likelihood based engine. The system dynamically mixes static datasets and on policy model rollouts inside a single trainer loop.\n\nIn practice, this eliminates the catastrophic forgetting and brittle distribution shifts that frequently plague post training handoffs. Controlled benchmarks show that unified training improves the Pareto frontier between compute expenditure and downstream task accuracy while maintaining broader base capabilities.\n\nFor infrastructure teams managing complex multi stage distributed clusters, collapsing these disjoint steps simplifies checkpointing and scheduling. The training problem shifts from coordinating bespoke algorithm transitions to curating data mixture ratios over time.\n\nModel alignment does not require an entirely distinct optimization paradigm; it is simply likelihood fitting with dynamically sampled targets."
    },
    {
