      "image": null,
      "content": "Engine optimization often looks like dark magic from the outside, but it usually comes down to disciplined profiling and understanding hardware trade-offs. In game engines and high-throughput rendering systems, CPU bottlenecks will starve the GPU regardless of how optimized your shaders are.\n\nThe Godot team demonstrates this balance clearly. While 3D workloads frequently bind on the GPU, 2D pipelines almost always bottleneck on the CPU due to draw call overhead. Godot uses CPU-side batching to save thousands of driver dispatches, accepting minor GPU work to relieve massive CPU pressure.\n\nThe core takeaway is that optimization is never about raw instruction count in isolation. It is about profiling your critical path and shifting work across execution boundaries to ensure no single processor stalls the entire pipeline."
    },
    {
      "title": "Evaluating AI agents requires grading actions rather than replies",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.evalshift.dev/blog/ai-evals-for-agents",
