      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50340276",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Browser-based virtual machines typically struggle with networking because web browsers cannot open raw TCP or UDP sockets. Running an isolated operating system or a user-space network stack inside WebAssembly requires bridging Ethernet packets across an application-layer boundary.\n\nRelaynet addresses this by tunneling raw Ethernet frames over WebSocket connections to a lightweight relay service. The relay unpacks these frames and routes outbound IPv4 TCP and UDP traffic directly to the public internet, while maintaining isolated virtual private networks for sessions under the same account.\n\nDecoupling the virtual network stack from host kernel interfaces makes sandboxed compute environments significantly more capable. Engineers building client-side developer tooling, isolated test runners, or browser-hosted runtimes can now support standard networking protocols without operating complex custom VPN tunnels."
    },
    {
      "title": "Simple agentic loops can outperform complex theorem proving workflows",
