      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50344986",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Over-engineered agent frameworks often fail to beat a simple feedback loop paired with scaled test-time compute. A research paper evaluating automated theorem proving in Lean 4 shows that minimal agent harnesses can outperform elaborate multi-step pipelines.\n\nMost automated formal verification tools rely on rigid decomposition, specialized planners, and complex workflow orchestrators. In contrast, the h1 harness simply connects the language model to Lean 4, feeding back compiler diagnostics and verifier errors so the model can self-reflect.\n\nWithout any custom planning logic, this minimalist loop established state-of-the-art results across benchmarks such as PutnamBench and solved eight open mathematical problems. The authors found that an efficient, low-latency interface to the verification environment mattered far more than bespoke agent architecture.\n\nWhen you scale inference compute against an unyielding ground-truth verifier, generic agent iteration reliably defeats hand-crafted heuristics."
    },
    {
